Still curious, if we say,

 p <- ggplot(...

Then what is p if not a pointer to a graph object?  No, I'm not getting this from SAS, but my formal computer science training, such as Pascal (which was about the the time S+ was written so I see similarities).  I've taught computer science classes, such as data structures courses about queues.   Am not as interested in SAS-like by statements as I am in creating a vector of graphs, such as p[1], p[2], for example 50 state graphs.  But the below example actually calls the code to create the graph, rather than dealing with saved graphs each referenced by a vector element.

Is it not possible to have a vector of graph objects?  Once can say list(p,q,r), but I'm not sure what that graph object is if it is not a pointer.

On the other thread, will try the double brackets in the morning and let you know.  I am animating with saveHTML, but would rather have my graph objects created and tested rather than to call a function to create them right in the saveHTML(), but all the examples of doing this show calling the function that actually creates graphs, not cycling through existing graphs.

-Mary

--- djm...@gmail.com wrote:

From: Dennis Murphy <djm...@gmail.com>
To: mlho...@avalon.net
Cc: ggp...@googlegroups.com
Subject: Re: How to declare a vectors of pointers to ggplot graphs?
Date: Sun, 3 Feb 2013 16:22:53 -0800

Hi:

I'm not quite sure in which direction you're headed, but maybe the
following toy example will help. It's not perfect, but it illustrates
the idea.

library(plyr)
library(maps)

ia <- map_data("county", "iowa")
mn <- map_data("county", "minnesota")
wi <- map_data("county", "wisconsin")
state_data <- list(ia, mn, wi)

mid_range <- function(x) mean(range(x))

# Not perfect - spits out more output than intended
plotfun <- function(DF)
{
   require(plyr)
   require(ggplot2)

   seats <- ddply(DF, .(subregion), colwise(mid_range, .(lat, long)))

   p <- ggplot(DF, aes(long, lat)) +
         geom_polygon( aes(group = group),
                         fill = "lightyellow", color = "grey60") +
         geom_text( data = seats, aes(label = subregion), size = 2, angle = 45 )

   p
}

# Apply plotfun to each state's data, save in the list state_seats
state_seats <- llply(state_data, plotfun)

# apply the print() function to each ggplot object in state_seats
# (this is what needs work, as you'll find when you call it)
llply(state_seats, function(x) print(x))
## or somewhat more verbose in output,
## llply(state_seats, print)

Basic idea:

 * Combine the input data sets into a list.
 * Write a single function that outputs a ggplot object for an
arbitrary input data frame.
 * Use a **ply family function to recursively generate the ggplot
objects from the components of the input list; save to another list
object.
 * Use another **ply family function to print it.

At a basic level, there is no concept of 'pointers' in R (they exist,
but matter only when doing advanced programming). The above is more
the 'R-way' of doing things - the SAS analogue would be the BY
statement.

HTH,
Dennis

