Thanks so much to Ista and Dennis and everyone who contributed!

Now I can test out my plots such as this:

q <- list(27)

p <- ggplot(...)
q[[1]] <- p

And then in the animation I can play them back like this:

library(animation)
saveHTML(
{
for (i in 1:27)
{
   print(q[[i]])
}
}
, img.name <http://img.name> = "test", single.opts = "'utf8': false", autoplay = FALSE,
    interval = 0.5, imgdir = "claimsplot_dir", htmlfile = "test.html",
    ani.height = 600, ani.width = 800, title = "Test",
    outdir=getwd())

-Mary

p.s. I think I also learned a lot about R in the lively discussion.  I think the most important point was that when we assign a graph to a variable like p, we are saving the instructions on how to create the graph rather than the graph itself.  So they are not pointers, but rather they are lists with the information needed to create the graph.

