Alpha is not a good enough solution because then the key does not make
sense. I know I can't reorder a factor for each bar, hence the
problem. There needs to be some built in way to make overlapping bar
charts work better. The current implementation is stupid. What
possible benefit is there to having overlapping bars if they are just
going to hide data?

On Tue, Feb 12, 2013 at 2:44 PM, Brandon Hurr <brando...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Give them an alpha and they'll all show up. The issue is if your factor
> levels are always in the same order of quantity or not. If they are, you can
> easily do this, if they aren't... I don't believe you can reorder a factor
> for each bar. You could do some fancy grid editing, but it isn't worth it
> unless you really need it. Consider an alternative visualization method?
>
>
> On Tue, Feb 12, 2013 at 7:02 AM, Ryan Hope <rmh...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> Simple example:
>>
>>
>> ggplot(data.frame(x=rep(c("A","B"),each=3),y=rep(c("X","Y","Z"),times=2),z=c(1,2,3,2,3,1)))
>> + geom_bar(aes(x=x,y=z,fill=y),position="dodge")
>>
>>
>> ggplot(data.frame(x=rep(c("A","B"),each=3),y=rep(c("X","Y","Z"),times=2),z=c(1,2,3,2,3,1)))
>> + geom_bar(aes(x=x,y=z,fill=y),position="identity")
>>
>> In the second example much of the data is hidden.
>>
>> On Tue, Feb 12, 2013 at 1:47 AM, Ryan Hope <rmh...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> > Actually, position_identity() does not do what you what I want it to.
>> > The bars are overlapped but they are not layered in a way that makes
>> > all the fill factors visible.
>> >
>> > On Tue, Feb 12, 2013 at 12:15 AM, Ryan Hope <rmh...@gmail.com> wrote:
