>
> Best,
> Ista
>
> On Thu, Feb 14, 2013 at 2:16 PM, Ista Zahn <ista...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Jennifer,
> >
> > Yep, should be no problem:
> >
> > sevals$variable <- factor("avgmass",
> >                           levels = levels(combo2012$variable))
> > combo2012 <- merge(combo2012, sevals, all=TRUE)
> > combo2012 <- within(combo2012, {
> >   lwr <- value - 1.96*massse
> >   upr <- value + 1.96*massse
> > })
> >
> > ggplot (data=combo2012, aes(x = date,y=value,group=variable)) +
> >   geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lwr, ymax=upr)) +
> >   geom_line() +
> >   scale_x_date(limits = c(as.Date("2012-8-16"), as.Date("2012-11-15")))+
> >   facet_grid(variable ~ ., scale = "free_y")
> >
> > Best,
> > Ista
> >
> > On Thu, Feb 14, 2013 at 11:34 AM, Jennifer Krauel
> > <unquench...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >> I want to print relevant variables for one field season over time.  I'm
> >> comparing the amount of north wind, number of moths caught in traps, mass of
> >> the local bats, and the number of hours those bats are foraging.  You'll see
> >> that some dates are missing for some variables; bats were not sampled every
> >> day, for example, but ggplot handles that appropriately when I used na=rm in
> >> the melt.   In the code below, the first data frame contains the basic data
> >> for all the variables of interest.
> >>
> >> One of these variables is an average (daily bat mass). The second data frame
> >> below contains the standard error for bat mass, one of the variables.  Since
> >> I've melted the variables, I don't think it is possible to put the std error
> >> values into that first melted data frame.
